## 2. Non-negotiable result

Production must have one transaction ownership authority:

    raw transaction input
      -> validation evidence
      -> read-only Plan
      -> one total Apply in TxPoolAuthority
      -> committed Effect
      -> external endpoint

`AuthorityStore` owns all `PreAccepted`, `Accepted`, and
`ReplacementHistory` transaction locations plus the exact paired chain
snapshot. Indexes, scheduler order, resource use, dependency levels, template
receipts, RPC views, persistence receipts, metrics, caches, and effects are
derived from this authority. None may decide transaction membership.

The production cutover is invalid if it leaves any request able to mutate both
the UAK and a legacy `TxPool`, `PrePool`, or `EffectJournal`, or if different
requests use different membership authorities. There is no shadow dual-write,
fallback write path, repair scan, or gradual endpoint-by-endpoint ownership
switch.

## 3. Why the cutover cannot be piecemeal

The current service simultaneously constructs the legacy accepted `TxPool`,
the legacy `component::pre_pool::PrePool`, and the legacy
`service::effects::EffectJournal`. The UAK already models both accepted and
preaccepted ownership. Replacing RPC or worker calls one by one would create a
temporary three-authority system whose cross-product cannot be proven atomic.

Safe migration therefore has two implementation zones:

1. Before cutover, new code is reachable only through isolated UAK tests and
   typed UAK APIs. Legacy production behavior remains unchanged.
2. One coordinated assembly switch makes all production mutation and coherent
   reads use one `AuthorityRuntime`; the now-unreachable legacy production
   topology is deleted or disconnected in the same phase.

Public compatibility types may remain when removing them would be an unrelated
API break. They must not be instantiated as a production membership owner.

